<p>The Northern Pacific Railroad announced in 1879 that it would extend its line 200 miles west to the Yellowstone River near present-day Glendive, Montana. Ferrying supplies across the Missouri River was an expensive temporary measure; a bridge across the "Big Muddy" was necessary to complete a northern transcontinental rail route.</p>

<p>The Missouri River narrowed north of Bismarck, and construction of a levee could narrow it further. Even so, three 400-foot spans were needed between land-supported approaches at each end. Announcement of the project brought thousands of workers to the area and helped determine the location of the work camp that became Mandan.</p>

<p>The name "High Bridge" refers to its 70-foot clearance above the river, intended to protect the structure from widely fluctuating water levels and the enormous sheets of ice common on the upper Missouri in early spring. Designer {% person "george-s-morison" %}George S. Morison{% endperson %} also placed metal sheaths, or blades, on the upstream sides of the piers to break apart the ice.</p>

<p>The bridge rests on piles driven into the sand and riverbed and on stone piers supporting the deck. Workers built the piers using timber-and-iron caissons measuring 74 by 25 by 17 feet. Each caisson was floated into position and sunk. Concrete poured above a false floor pushed it into the riverbed. Workers entered a hollow section at the bottom through an air lock and excavated beneath it until the caisson reached a firm layer. Granite piers were then built on the caisson foundations.</p>

